================================================================================
 PakkaHisaab — Azure Cost & Availability Analysis (Min Required Paid Plan)
 Region: Central India | Prepared: 2026-07-24 | Pricing source: Azure Retail
 Prices API (prices.azure.com), live-queried, INR, Consumption rates.
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. READ THIS FIRST — on "100% availability"
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
No Azure service, and no cloud provider, sells or contractually guarantees
100% uptime. Every Azure SLA document (App Service, SQL Database, everything)
is a percentage below 100%, paired with a *service-credit* remedy — if Microsoft
misses the number, you get a partial bill credit, not compensation for the
outage itself, and not a promise it won't happen again.

What this document gives you instead: the highest SLA Microsoft actually
publishes for each component in this plan, the combined ceiling for the whole
stack, and what it would cost to push closer to (never reach) 100% if you ever
need that. Treat "100%" as the marketing rounding of "as close as this budget
gets" — the real number below is 99.94%.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PER-ITEM PRICING — Min Required Paid Plan
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All rates fetched live from https://prices.azure.com/api/retail/prices,
armRegionName=centralindia, currencyCode=INR, 2026-07-24.

  ITEM 1 — App Service Plan (compute for BOTH API and Admin)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    Resource        : plan-pakkahisaab (already provisioned)
    Hosts            : api-pakkahisaab-amitagrawal + admin-pakkahisaab-amitagrawal
                        (2 sites on 1 plan — Admin adds ₹0 extra)
    SKU              : B1 Basic, Linux
    Meter            : "Azure App Service Basic Plan - Linux" / B1
    Rate             : Rs. 1.6991 / hour
    Billing model    : Consumption (always-on, 24x7 metering — Basic tier has
                        no auto-pause; it runs whether or not it's used)
    Max hours/month  : 744 (31-day month — this is the ceiling, not the average)
    MONTHLY MAX      : Rs. 1,264.34

  ITEM 2 — Azure SQL Database (data for API + Admin, same DB)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    Resource        : sql-pakkahisaab-amitagrawal / PakkaHisaab
    Recommended SKU  : Basic (5 DTU, 2 GB max storage)
    Meter            : "SQL Database Single Basic" / B DTU
    Rate             : Rs. 17.3779 / day
    Billing model    : Consumption, always-on, no auto-pause (this is the
                        change from your CURRENT config — see section 4)
    Max days/month   : 31
    MONTHLY MAX      : Rs. 538.71

  ITEM 3 — Admin console compute
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    Rs. 0 — shares Item 1's App Service Plan (numberOfSites: 2). No separate
    App Service Plan is needed or recommended; splitting it would only add a
    second B1 bill for zero functional benefit at this scale.

  ITEM 4 — Bandwidth / egress
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    First ~100 GB/month outbound is free in most Azure regions. This app's
    sync payloads are small delta batches (see the user-capacity analysis
    from this session) — realistically Rs. 0/month at anywhere near the
    200-300 household range this plan supports.

  ITEM 5 — Backup storage (Azure SQL point-in-time restore)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    Free allowance = 100% of provisioned database size (2 GB for Basic tier).
    At light usage this stays within the free allowance: Rs. 0/month.
    (Flag: grows if the SyncBatches table isn't purged — see section 5.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. TOTAL MONTHLY PRICE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Item                                   Rate            Monthly MAX (INR)
    ------------------------------------   -------------   ------------------
    App Service Plan B1 (API + Admin)      Rs 1.6991/hr    Rs   1,264.34
    Azure SQL Database Basic (5 DTU/2GB)   Rs 17.3779/day  Rs     538.71
    Admin console (shared plan)            -               Rs       0.00
    Bandwidth (est., within free tier)     -               Rs       0.00
    Backup storage (est., within free)     -               Rs       0.00
    ------------------------------------   -------------   ------------------
    TOTAL MONTHLY MAX                                      Rs   1,803.05
    ------------------------------------   -------------   ------------------
    ~ USD equivalent (approx, FX varies)                   ~ $21-22 / month

  Why "MAX" and not a flat number: both meters are hourly/daily consumption,
  so the bill scales with the days in that calendar month.
    30-day month actual : ~Rs 1,780.83
    31-day month (MAX)  : Rs 1,803.05   <- use this as your budget ceiling
    28-day month (Feb)  : ~Rs 1,657.75

  ANNUAL COST (12 months, using each month's real day-count, not 12x the max):
    Rs 1,803.05 (x7 months of 31 days) + Rs 1,780.83 (x4 months of 30 days)
    + Rs 1,657.75 (1 month of 28 days)
    ~= Rs 21,343 / year  (~ $257 / year)
    Budget ceiling if you want a single round number: Rs 21,700/year.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. AVAILABILITY ANALYSIS — the closest to "100%" this plan gets
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Component SLAs (Microsoft-published, current):
    App Service, Basic tier, single or multi-instance : 99.95%
      -> allowed downtime: 8,760 hrs/yr x 0.05% = 4.38 hours/year
    Azure SQL Database, Basic tier, single database    : 99.99%
      -> allowed downtime: 8,760 hrs/yr x 0.01% = 0.88 hours/year

  COMBINED CEILING (API needs both to be up -> multiply uptime, not add):
    0.9995 x 0.9999 = 0.99940  ->  99.94%
    Allowed combined downtime  :  ~5.3 hours / year  (~26 minutes / month)

  This 99.94% is the CONTRACTUAL ceiling for the plan priced in section 1 —
  the actual observed uptime is usually better, but nothing above this is
  promised, and Microsoft's only remedy for missing it is a bill credit
  (typically 10-25% of that month's charge for the affected service),
  not prevention or compensation for your own downtime/business impact.

  What is NOT covered by these SLAs (i.e. can still cause an outage this
  plan doesn't protect against):
    - The incident that started this conversation: a paused/free-tier SQL
      DB is a CONFIGURATION state, not a platform outage — no SLA credit
      would have applied, because the DB was technically "available," just
      configured to pause. This plan removes that specific failure mode by
      moving off the free/serverless auto-pause model entirely (Item 2).
    - Your own app-level bugs, bad deploys, DNS issues, client-side crashes.
    - Regional Azure outages (Central India as a whole going down) — both
      items live in ONE region with no failover. This is the biggest gap
      between 99.94% and "actually always up."

  TO PUSH CLOSER TO 100% (optional — NOT included in the min plan, priced
  separately, roughly DOUBLES the bill):
    - Second App Service (B1) + SQL Active Geo-Replication secondary in a
      second region (e.g. South India) + Azure Front Door/Traffic Manager
      for failover routing.
    - Rough added cost: + Rs 1,264 (2nd App Service) + Rs 539 (2nd DB
      replica) + Front Door (usage-based, ~Rs 3,000-4,000/mo at this
      traffic volume) = approx +Rs 4,800-5,800/month, ~Rs 6,600-7,600/month
      total. Still not 100% — regional failover typically adds a "nine"
      at best (~99.99% combined with correct automated failover), and
      requires engineering work this document doesn't scope or estimate.
    - Given the capacity analysis (this plan comfortably serves 200-300
      households), this spend is very unlikely to be justified until well
      past that scale.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. CHANGE FROM CURRENT STATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Current (as of this session, post-fix): SQL Database on General Purpose
  Serverless (GP_S_Gen5, 0.5-2 vCore, autoPauseDelay 60 min,
  freeLimitExhaustionBehavior=BillOverUsage). This is usage-billed and can
  be CHEAPER in a light-traffic month than the flat Rs 538.71 Basic-tier
  price above, but:
    - It still auto-pauses after 60 idle minutes (now safe to auto-resume
      since billing is allowed, but every pause still costs ~30-60s of
      cold-start latency on the next request).
    - Its SLA is the same 99.99% tier-wide, so no availability difference.
    - Its cost is NOT capped the way Basic tier's flat rate is — a busy
      month billed per-vCore-second could exceed Rs 538.71 (see the
      Serverless-always-on comparison from the earlier capacity analysis:
      Rs 54.18/vCore-hour, dramatically more than Basic if it stays warm).
  This document prices Basic tier because "min required PAID plan" was
  interpreted as the cheapest PREDICTABLE, always-on, no-cold-start
  configuration — not the cheapest possible bill in a quiet month.
  No infrastructure change has been made based on this document; the
  SQL tier switch from Serverless to Basic is a proposal, pending your go-ahead.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. OPEN ITEM AFFECTING BOTH PRICE AND AVAILABILITY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `PurgeOldSyncBatches` (named in DEPLOYMENT.md's hardening checklist as a
  daily Elastic Job) is NOT currently implemented. The SyncBatches table
  grows unbounded with every sync push. Left unchecked, it is the fastest
  route to hitting the Basic tier's 2 GB storage ceiling, which would cause
  write failures — a real availability risk this pricing plan does not
  protect against. Recommend implementing this before scaling past a few
  dozen active households, independent of which SQL tier is chosen.

